            <ArticleTitle>Tuberculosis Treated by Multiple Drugs: An overview.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2174/1567201814666171120125916</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Current tuberculosis (TB) treatment is established on an amalgamation of drugs that were evolved mostly in the central decades of the hind century. Cure rates are elevated for drug-sensitive strains of Mycobacterium tuberculosis (M.Tb) when the recommended complex and prolonged treatment protocols adhere. However, the difficulty in accurately prescribing and adhering to these protocols and the emergence of M. Tb strains resistant to multiple drugs and drug-drug interactions that interfere with optimal treatment of Tuberculosis and co-infected patients with the different disease has generated a pressing need for improved Tuberculosis therapies. Together with the ominous global burden of Tuberculosis, these shortcomings of current medication have contributed to a renewed interest in the development of improved drugs and protocols for the medication of Tuberculosis. This article highlights hurdles related to the optimized use of existing drugs and challenges related to the development of Modern, improved products, focusing in particular on aspects inherent in Tuberculosis drug clinical development. Concluding comments offered processes for more efficient development of Tuberculosis therapies.</AbstractText>
